{"documentId":"tos.work.sextus-empiricus.outlines-of-pyrrhonism","versionId":"retained-outlines-en-forage2026-ai","revision":"5796826c1e9b123b1253409ad3056e735c353f4f4bf94b1bdf84be7251587ff7","units":[{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-2940f02244e64645ae5181e1ec72dc81","ordinal":521,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.21","text":"\n\nBut neither can we apprehend the effect of a cause as its effect unless we apprehend the cause of the effect as its cause. We think we know that it is its effect only when we apprehend its cause as its cause."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-311d171da4d244769e2c9d08d3489ba1","ordinal":522,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.22","text":"\n\nSo, to conceive of the cause we must first recognize the effect, and to know the effect, ⟨as⟩ I said, we must first know the cause. If that is so, the mode of reciprocity shows both to be inconceivable: neither the cause can be conceived as a cause nor the effect as an effect. Since each of them needs the other to be made credible, we shall have no way of telling which of them to start from in forming the concept. Hence we shall not be able to state that anything is a cause of anything."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-250a1f969afd4375b79c013d19c92077","ordinal":523,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.23","text":"\n\nAnd even if someone grants that cause can be conceived, it would be thought inapprehensible because of the disagreement. For some say ⟨that something is a cause of something, others that it is not, others… For whoever says⟩ that ⟨something⟩ is a cause of something either says so simply, prompted by no reasonable cause, or will claim that he arrived at this assent because of certain causes. If he says so simply, he will be no more credible than someone who says simply that nothing is a cause of anything. If he states causes on account of which he thinks that ⟨something⟩ is a cause of something, he will be trying to establish what is in question by means of what is in question. For when we are investigating whether anything is a cause of anything, he says that there is a cause on the grounds that there is a cause of there being a cause."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-c36f5aab2e994e68a922c627d2c564ea","ordinal":524,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.24","text":"\n\nBesides, since we are investigating whether cause exists, he will certainly have to provide a cause for the cause of there being a cause, and another for that one, and so on to infinity. But it is impossible to provide infinitely many causes; therefore it is impossible to state firmly that anything is a cause of anything."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-4cb53da828bb457d8fa89e826293242f","ordinal":525,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.25","text":"\n\nMoreover, the cause produces the effect either when it already exists and is real as a cause, or when it is not a cause. When it is not, certainly not. If when it is, it must first be real and have come into being as a cause, and only then bring on the effect, which is said to be produced by it when it already is a cause. But since a cause is relative, and relative to the effect, it clearly cannot be real as its cause before it. So not even when it is a cause can the cause produce that of which it is the cause."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-19d9f01c4fe74a9e959e561c20bcbca3","ordinal":526,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.26","text":"\n\nBut if it produces nothing either when it is not a cause or when it is, it does not produce anything at all; and so it will not be a cause either, since without producing something a cause cannot be thought of as a cause. Hence some also say this. The cause must either be real together with the effect, or be real before it, or ⟨exist⟩ after the ⟨effect⟩ comes about. Now to say that the cause is brought into existence after its effect has been generated may well be laughable. But neither can it be real before it. For it is said to be thought of relative to the effect, and relatives, as they themselves say,"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-e8b1b0b07d7f4afc9321dff4d22ad557","ordinal":527,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.27","text":"\n\nexist together and are thought of together, insofar as they are relatives. Nor can it be real together with the effect. For if it produces the effect, and what comes about must come about through something that already exists, the cause must first become a cause and then produce the effect. So the cause is neither real before its effect nor together with it, and ⟨the effect⟩ does not come about before it either. Perhaps, then, it has no share in existence at all."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-324b95b05c0b4fbfba7e00c1d95d1169","ordinal":528,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.28","text":"\n\nIt is perhaps clear that through these arguments too the concept of cause turns itself around again. As a relative, the cause cannot be conceived before its effect. Yet in order to be conceived as the cause of its effect, it must be conceived before its effect. And it is impossible for a thing to be conceived before that before which [a thing] cannot be conceived. So it is impossible for the cause to be conceived."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-2bfd62e603c84749a5071eb045900ad3","ordinal":529,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.29","text":"\n\nFrom all this, then, we conclude as follows. ⟨Suppose⟩ the arguments by which we pointed out that one should say there is cause are plausible. Suppose the arguments establishing that it is not proper to state that there is any cause are plausible too. And suppose it is not possible to prefer any of them, since we have no agreed sign, criterion or proof, as we established earlier. Then it is necessary to suspend judgment about the existence of cause as well, saying that there is “no more” a cause than not, as far as what the dogmatists say goes."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-c892365863694fadb4987cbceaae3b10","ordinal":530,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.30","text":"\n\nThat much will be enough to have said for now about the active principle. But we should also speak briefly about the so-called material principles. That these are inapprehensible, ungraspable, is easy to see from the disagreement there has been about them among the dogmatists. Pherecydes of Syros said that earth is the principle of all things; Thales of Miletus, water; Anaximander, his pupil, the unlimited; Anaximenes and Diogenes of Apollonia, air; Hippasus of Metapontum, fire; Xenophanes of Colophon, earth and water; Oenopides of Chios, fire and air; Hippo of Rhegium, fire and water; Onomacritus in the Orphica, fire, water and earth;\n〔1〕 Most of these are early Greek natural philosophers of the sixth and fifth centuries BC; Onomacritus was a sixth-century BC compiler of poems attributed to Orpheus."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-bdedafa2f9ad4c7e8552287cf6081ecc","ordinal":531,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.31","text":"\n\nEmpedocles and his school, and the Stoics, fire, air, water and earth. (As for the matter without qualities that some tell marvelous tales about, which not even they firmly claim to apprehend, why even mention it?) Aristotle the Peripatetic and his school said fire, air, water, earth and the body that moves in a circle;"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-6fa85c10586a4ba58690ad01a95bc06b","ordinal":532,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.32","text":"\n\nDemocritus and Epicurus, atoms; Anaxagoras of Clazomenae, homoeomeries; Diodorus, nicknamed Cronus, minimal and partless bodies; Heraclides of Pontus and Asclepiades of Bithynia, jointless masses; Pythagoras and his followers, numbers; the mathematicians, the limits of bodies; Strato the natural philosopher, qualities.\n〔1〕 Strato of Lampsacus headed Aristotle’s school, the Peripatetics, in the third century BC."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-5a3bb53224a9413fab4967d54a653d90","ordinal":533,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.33","text":"\n\nSo much disagreement has arisen among them about the material principles, and more besides. We shall therefore assent either to all the positions mentioned, and the others too, or to some of them. But assenting to all is not possible. We surely cannot assent at once to Asclepiades and his followers, who say that the elements are breakable and have qualities; to Democritus and his followers, who claim that they are indivisible and without qualities; and to Anaxagoras and his followers, who leave every perceptible quality in the homoeomeries."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-e74d60a0359347a0b667b0d8bc81b20e","ordinal":534,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.34","text":"\n\nAnd if we prefer one position over the others, we shall prefer it either simply and without proof, or with proof. Without proof we shall not accept it. If with proof, the proof must be true. But a proof could not be granted to be true unless it had been judged by a true criterion, and a criterion is shown to be true by means of a judged proof."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-5c316e2c01434f2e8b5ed2b938520b03","ordinal":535,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.35","text":"\n\nSo, for the proof that prefers some position to be shown true, its criterion must have been proved; and for the criterion to be proved, its proof must have been judged beforehand. If that is so, we find the mode of reciprocity, which will not let the argument advance, since the proof always needs a proved criterion and the criterion a judged proof."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-6c72505fb7ce4e1697eda812e2728aee","ordinal":536,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.36","text":"\n\nAnd if someone wanted always to judge a criterion by a criterion and prove a proof by a proof, he will be thrown into infinite regress. So if we can neither assent to all the positions about elements nor to any one of them, it is proper to suspend judgment about them."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-c0d10054019148eeae996472baac67b9","ordinal":537,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.37","text":"\n\nPerhaps it is possible to point out the non-apprehension of the elements and material principles through these considerations alone. But so that we can refute the dogmatists more comprehensively, let us spend a reasonable amount of time on the topic. As we pointed out, the opinions about elements are many, almost infinite. Because of the character of this treatise, we shall excuse ourselves for now from addressing each of them specifically, but in effect we shall argue against all of them. Whatever position anyone states about elements will come down either to bodies or to incorporeals. So we think it is enough to point out that bodies are inapprehensible and that incorporeals are inapprehensible. This will make it clear that the elements are inapprehensible too."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-c80abf512a884909a63f68197ee02523","ordinal":538,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.38","text":"\n\nSome say that a body is what is capable of acting or being acted upon. But as far as this concept goes, body is inapprehensible. For cause is inapprehensible, as we pointed out. And since we cannot say whether there is any cause, we cannot say whether there is anything acted upon either, for what is acted upon is certainly acted upon by a cause. Since both cause and what is acted upon are inapprehensible, body too will be inapprehensible for these reasons."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-88664fccadcf4de595e9a87cb02e56be","ordinal":539,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.39","text":"\n\nOthers say that a body is what has three dimensions together with resistance. A point, they say, is that which has no part; a line is length without breadth; a surface is length with breadth. When the surface also takes on depth and resistance, it is a body, made up of length, breadth, depth and resistance. Body is the subject of our present discussion."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-ea0e8ebe5c8c4112925bd44afdc1e646","ordinal":540,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.40","text":"\n\nThe argument against these people is easy too. They will say either that body is nothing besides these things or that it is something other than the combination of them. Now apart from length, breadth, depth and resistance, body would be nothing. But if these things are body, then anyone who showed that they are unreal would abolish body too, for wholes are abolished along with all their parts. There are various ways of refuting these things, but for now it will be enough to say this: if the limits exist, they are either lines, or surfaces, or bodies."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-d575d6b205e34a2693284fc5a022429f","ordinal":541,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.41","text":"\n\nSuppose someone says that there is a surface or a line. Then it will be said either that each of the things mentioned can be real on its own, or that they are only observed in connection with what are called bodies. But to dream up a line or a surface existing by itself is perhaps simple-minded. And suppose each of them were said to be observed only in connection with bodies, and not to be real by itself. First, it will straightaway be granted that bodies have not come into being from them (for these ought, I suppose, first to have had existence by themselves, and then to have come together and made the bodies);"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-17538182fbb743639f12f1469bb7b879","ordinal":542,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.42","text":"\n\nand then, they are not real even in so-called bodies. This can be pointed out in many ways, but for now it will be enough to state the difficulties raised by contact. If bodies placed side by side touch one another, they make contact by their limits, by their surfaces, for instance. Now the surfaces will not be wholly united with one another through and through when they are in contact, since then contact would be fusion, and separating things in contact would be tearing them apart. That is not what we observe."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-613f36cd8e1749659ece424e08d9e50b","ordinal":543,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.43","text":"\n\nSuppose the surface touches the surface of the body placed beside it with ⟨some⟩ of its parts, and is united by others with the body whose limit it is. ⟨Then it will not be without depth, since its parts are thought of as different in depth: one touching the adjacent body, the other being where it is united with the body whose limit it is.⟩ So no one can observe length and breadth without depth even in connection with a body, and hence no surface either. Similarly, suppose two surfaces are placed side by side along the limits in which they end, that is, along what is said to be their length: along lines. These lines, by which the surfaces are said to touch one another, will not be united with one another (for they would be fused). And if each of them touches the line placed beside it with some of its parts in breadth, and is united by others with the surface whose limit it is, it will not be without breadth, and hence it will not be a line. And if there is neither line nor surface in a body, there will be no length or breadth or depth in a body either."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-936ba60a72a747e69500f30fd7cfe9c0","ordinal":544,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.44","text":"\n\nIf someone says that the limits are bodies, the answer to him will be short. If length is a body, it will have to be divided into its three dimensions. Each of these, being a body, will itself be divided in turn into three other dimensions, which will be bodies; and those likewise into others, and so on to infinity. The body would then become infinitely large, being divided into infinitely many parts, which is absurd. So the dimensions mentioned are not bodies either. And if they are neither bodies nor lines nor surfaces, they will not be thought to exist at all."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-f65ba484509747908b93fabfbf86f414","ordinal":545,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.45","text":"\n\nResistance, too, is inapprehensible. If it is apprehended, it would be apprehended by touch. So if we show that touch is inapprehensible, it will be clear that resistance cannot be apprehended. That touch is inapprehensible we infer as follows. Things that touch each other touch either part to part or whole to whole. Whole to whole, certainly not, for then they would be united and would not touch each other. Nor part to part. For their parts are parts in relation to the wholes, but wholes in relation to their own parts. So these wholes, ⟨which⟩ are parts of other things, will not touch whole to whole"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-4608807bf56e4c18847b541a5aad571e","ordinal":546,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.46","text":"\n\nfor the reasons just given, nor part to part. For their parts too are wholes in relation to their own parts, and so will touch neither whole to whole nor part to part. And if we do not apprehend contact taking place either as a whole or by parts, touch will be inapprehensible. For this reason resistance will be too, and hence body too. For if body is nothing besides the three dimensions and resistance, and we have shown that each of these is inapprehensible, body too will be inapprehensible. Thus, as far as the concept of body goes, it is inapprehensible whether there is any body."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-9daab9c4bb7347eca946faa5e780aa0f","ordinal":547,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.47","text":"\n\nThis too should be said on the topic at hand. Of the things that exist, they say, some are perceptible and some intelligible. Some are apprehended by thought and others by the senses. The senses, they say, are affected only in a simple way, while thought proceeds from the apprehension of perceptibles to the apprehension of intelligibles. If there is any body, then, it is either perceptible or intelligible. It is not perceptible. It seems to be apprehended through an aggregate of length, depth, breadth, resistance, color and certain other things, along with which it is observed; but they themselves say that the senses are affected only in a simple way."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-f1f4f65dfac947538941d6db100d7cbf","ordinal":548,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.48","text":"\n\n⟨....⟩ But if body is said to exist, there must certainly be something perceptible in the nature of things from which the conception of bodies, intelligible as they are, will arise. But there is nothing besides body and the incorporeal. Of these, the incorporeal is intelligible from the start, and body is not perceptible, as we pointed out. So, since there is nothing perceptible in the nature of things from which the conception of body could arise, body will not be intelligible either. And if it is neither perceptible nor intelligible, and there is nothing besides these, we must say, as far as the argument goes, that body is nothing."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-3b2c93cf3a1e4383b751d2b231b70c67","ordinal":549,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.49","text":"\n\nFor these reasons, then, we set the arguments against body against the fact that body appears [to seem] real, and so we arrive at suspension of judgment about body. The non-apprehension of body brings with it the inapprehensibility of the incorporeal. For privations are thought of as privations of states: blindness of sight, deafness of hearing, and similarly in other cases. So in order to apprehend a privation, we must already have apprehended the state of which the privation is said to be a privation. Someone who had no concept of sight could not say that this person does not have sight, which is what being blind is."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-644e269c8c89415da25f215b53440d09","ordinal":550,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.50","text":"\n\nSo the incorporeal is a privation of body. Privations cannot be apprehended if the states are not apprehended, and it has been shown that body is inapprehensible. If all this holds, the incorporeal will be inapprehensible too. Moreover, it is either perceptible or intelligible. If it is perceptible, it is inapprehensible because of the differences among animals, among humans, among the senses and among circumstances, because of admixtures, and because of the rest of what we said in discussing the Ten Modes. If it is intelligible, there is a difficulty. The apprehension of perceptibles is not granted from the start, and it is from perceptibles that we seem to reach out to intelligibles. So the apprehension of intelligibles will not be granted from the start either, and hence neither will that of the incorporeal."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-8ca8e148b1e34f0aa49f943c56c768cd","ordinal":551,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.51","text":"\n\nAnyone who claims to apprehend the incorporeal will make the case that he apprehends it either by perception or by argument. Not by perception, certainly. The senses seem to grasp perceptibles by pressure and impact. Take sight, whether it happens by the stretching of a cone, or by images being emitted and received, or by outpourings of rays or colors. Or take hearing, whether it is the air that is struck or the particles of the sound that travel to the ears and strike the auditory breath, so as to produce the perception of the sound. Odors, again, strike the nose and flavors the tongue, and likewise the things that move touch strike touch."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-a2100b758c6946218233ceec01e86456","ordinal":552,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.52","text":"\n\nBut incorporeals cannot undergo pressure of this kind, so ⟨they⟩ could not be apprehended by perception. Nor by argument either. If argument is a sayable and incorporeal, as the Stoics say, whoever says that incorporeals are apprehended by argument is taking for granted the point at issue. For when we are investigating whether anything incorporeal can be apprehended, he simply takes something incorporeal and wants to use it to apprehend incorporeals. Yet argument itself, if it is incorporeal, is one of the things under investigation."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-a4b36440008141c9ba4c524c19744047","ordinal":553,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.53","text":"\n\nHow, then, will anyone prove that this incorporeal (I mean argument) is apprehended in the first place? If through another incorporeal, we shall ask for the proof that that one is apprehended too, and so on to infinity. If through a body, the apprehension of bodies is also under investigation. So through what shall we show that the body is apprehended, the body we use to prove that incorporeal argument is apprehended? If through a body, we are thrown into infinite regress; if through an incorporeal, we fall into the mode of reciprocity. Since argument thus remains inapprehensible, if it is incorporeal, no one could say that the incorporeal is apprehended through it."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-e915d507d0214a1991bacf09e17361ca","ordinal":554,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.54","text":"\n\nAnd suppose argument is a body. There is disagreement about bodies too, whether they are apprehended or not, because of what is called their continual flux. For this reason they do not even admit of being pointed out as “this”, and are not thought to exist; that is why Plato calls bodies things that come into being but never are. So I am at a loss how the disagreement about the incorporeal will be decided. I see that it can be decided neither by a body nor by an incorporeal, because of the absurdities stated a little earlier. So it is not possible to apprehend incorporeals by argument either."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-74fe065a2726400b94fee3d069a82bb2","ordinal":555,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.55","text":"\n\nAnd if they neither fall under perception nor are apprehended through argument, they could not be apprehended at all. If, then, it is not possible to make firm assertions about the existence of body or of incorporeals, we must suspend judgment about the elements too. Perhaps we must also suspend judgment about the things that come after the elements, since some of them are bodies and others incorporeals, and both have been shown to be problematic. At any rate, since both the active and the material principles are subjects of suspension for these reasons, the account of principles ends in aporia."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-3eb988f59dbc48a1b37d79601050ccad","ordinal":556,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.56","text":"\n\nAnd even if one sets all this aside, how do they say that compounds come into being from the primary elements, when there is no touching or contact, and no blending or mixing at all? That contact is nothing I pointed out a little earlier, when I was discussing the existence of body. That the manner of blending is not possible either, as far as what they say goes, I shall indicate briefly. Much is said about blending, and the dogmatists hold almost endless positions on the question before us. So the inapprehensibility of the question might be concluded straightaway, along with the undecidable disagreement. But because of the aim of this treatise, we shall excuse ourselves for now from rebutting each of them, and we think it will be enough at present to say the following."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-81f2f16ee5dc40568a9048673ca534a4","ordinal":557,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.57","text":"\n\nThey say that things that are blended are composed of substance and qualities. So someone will say one of four things. Either their substances are mixed but their qualities are not at all; or their qualities are mixed but their substances are not; or neither is mixed with the other; or both are united with one another. But if neither the qualities nor the substances are mixed with one another, blending will be inconceivable. For how will a single perception arise from the things blended, if they are mixed with one another in none of the ways mentioned?"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-72d56a15d86342cebf61cff4bebf506b","ordinal":558,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.58","text":"\n\nIf the qualities were said simply to lie side by side with one another while the substances are mixed, that too would be absurd. We do not perceive the qualities in blends as separate; we perceive them as a single quality produced from the things blended. And if someone says the qualities are mixed but the substances not at all, he will be saying something impossible. The qualities have their existence in the substances, so it would be ridiculous to say that the qualities, separated from the substances, [and] are mixed with one another somewhere on their own, while the substances are left behind separately, without qualities."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-2418be87c7ae4619bf416cb00e68c8d9","ordinal":559,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.59","text":"\n\nIt remains to say that both the qualities and the substances of the things blended pass through one another and, by mixing, produce the blend. This is more absurd than the previous options, for such a blend is impossible. Suppose, for instance, that a cup of hemlock juice is mixed with ten cups of water. The hemlock would be said to be blended through all the water; at any rate, if you took even the tiniest part of the mixture, you would find it full of the hemlock’s power."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-8f14b14297a54d4bb95ad17e37142a87","ordinal":560,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.60","text":"\n\nSuppose, then, that the hemlock is mixed into every part of the water and extended along it, whole along whole, by the passage of their substances and qualities through one another, so that blending comes about in this way. And things extended along one another in every part occupy an equal space, and are therefore equal to one another. Then the one cup of hemlock will be equal to the ten cups of water, so that the mixture ought to be twenty cups, or only two, as far as this hypothesis about the manner of blending goes. And again, if a cup of water is added to the twenty cups, the measure ought to be forty cups, as far as the reasoning of the hypothesis goes, or again only two. For the one cup can be thought of as the twenty cups it is extended along, and the twenty cups as the one cup with which they are made equal.\n〔1〕 The cup here is the kotylē, a Greek liquid measure of roughly a quarter of a liter."}]}